  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 411 PM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n18Z RAP analysis and GOES visible imagery show a 994 mb low centered \nover the Northern Plains while sprawling high pressure dominates \nabove New England. This leaves a tightly packed pressure gradient \nsitting atop Lake Superior and the Upper Peninsula this afternoon. \nTemperatures in the eastern UP remain mostly below 70F, with \nsoutherly flow modified by cooler Lake Michigan SSTs and lingering \ncloud cover over the east. Most locations in the western UP have \nalready achieved temperatures in the low 80s, with some spots in the \nmid 80s. Likewise, relative humidities in the east range from 50%-\n60%, while those in the far west have dropped to 30%-40%. The \nupstream 12Z GRB sounding shows dry air (dewpoints to 0C) at 925 mb \nand very dry air (dewpoints to -20C) at 700 mb. SPC Mesoanalysis \nshows elevated 925 mb winds of up to 35kt over land areas. With \nprimed HDW conditions and a Red Flag Warning currently in effect for \nOntonagon and Gogebic counties and elevated fire conditions \nelsewhere, we continue to monitor for ongoing wildfires.\n\nAs the day reaches its period of peak mixing, expect to see breezy \nconditions intensify and relative humidities continue to drop. The \nmost recent REFS run continues to highlight areas of the far western \nand eastern UP, along with the Huron Mountains toward the Lake \nSuperior shoreline, with a 40-90% chance of seeing gusts to 40 mph \nwell into this evening. This is thanks largely to high temperatures \nallowing mixing with a strong LLJ (100th percentile at 50-60 kts at \n925 mb per the REFS) prior to nocturnal PBL decoupling. As \nobservations trend toward the low-end scenario of dryness, can \nlikewise anticipate relatively widespread RH's in the 30-35% range \nin the western UP to be achieved this early evening. It's a good \nidea to check with your local DNR restrictions this evening prior to \nlighting the campfire.\n\nTomorrow, the Northern Plains low is forecast to weaken and elongate \nrapidly between northern MN and toward James Bay, resulting in a \nswath of low pressure to the area's NNW. The pressure gradient eases \nup slightly tomorrow, though with continued WWA, anomalously high \ntemperatures persist through Monday, with highs and lows 15-30F \nabove normal the rule until Monday night. Lows tonight are forecast \nin the mid 50s and low 60s and highs tomorrow very similar to today, \nin the high 70s and low 80s. Winds diminish after late morning \ntomorrow, followed by lows again tomorrow night near 60F. Tuesday \nmarks a sharp break in the pattern, with the stretched low system \ndescending south across the CWA. Winds shift northeasterly, and \nprecipitation chances for a wetting rain Monday into Tuesday range \nbetween 35% and 50%. For later in the week, high pressure looks to \nmove in, though will note that LREF clusters show noticeable spread \nby only Day 4, so uncertainty remains high for that time period.",
